{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 40, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Raymond III Count of Tripoli also known as Raymond II of Marseille was a Count of Tripoli who reigned from 1152 until his death in 1187 He was the son of Bertrand Count of Toulouse and of Helen of Burgundy He succeeded his uncle Bertrand as Count of Tripoli in 1152 Raymond III was also the Count of Marseille by virtue of his father's possessions in Provence\n\nRaymond III was involved in the power struggles of the Kingdom of Jerusalem He was a claimant to the throne of Jerusalem after the death of Queen Melisende in 1161 He supported the claim of his cousin Baldwin III against that of Melisende's son Amalric I He was captured by Nur ad-Din the Zengid ruler of Aleppo and Damascus in 1164 and released in 1173 He played a prominent role in the defense of the Kingdom of Jerusalem against the forces of Saladin\n\nRaymond III married Eschiva of Saint Omer in 1174 They had three children Raymond IV Count of Tripoli Bohemond Count of Tripoli and Lucia of Tripoli who married Bohemond III Prince of Antioch\n\nRaymond III was a key figure in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem He was a rival of Reynald of Chatillon who was a powerful baron in the Kingdom of Jerusalem Raymond III opposed Reynald's aggressive policies against the Muslims and his alliance with the Byzantine Empire\n\nIn 1183 Raymond III was appointed regent of the Kingdom of Jerusalem by Baldwin IV He served as regent until the death of Baldwin IV in 1185 He continued to serve as regent for Baldwin V until the young king's death in 1186\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 at the Battle of Hattin He was one of the most prominent and capable leaders of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the 12th century His reign was marked by his efforts to defend the kingdom against the forces of Saladin and his involvement in the power struggles of the kingdom.\n\n*Early life and reign*\n---------------------